00:55:16.960 --> 00:55:18.240
and to try to give you that answer

00:55:18.240 --> 00:55:19.550
before somebody else does.

00:55:19.550 --> 00:55:21.400
- I was laying in bed,

00:55:21.400 --> 00:55:22.440
I think I was laying in bed,

00:55:22.440 --> 00:55:24.820
and this thought popped into
my head, I don't know why.

00:55:24.820 --> 00:55:27.570
Maybe I was reading something
on Reddit about Jeopardy.

00:55:29.970 --> 00:55:30.803
Unrelated to what I was reading,

00:55:30.803 --> 00:55:35.170
I was like why is it formatted

00:55:35.170 --> 00:55:39.060
that you answer, you
don't answer a question.

00:55:39.060 --> 00:55:42.040
You respond to an answer with a question.

00:55:42.040 --> 00:55:45.410
So I looked that up 'cause I
couldn't formulate a guess.

00:55:45.410 --> 00:55:48.080
I couldn't figure out why
that would be the case.

00:55:48.080 --> 00:55:49.630
- Did you formulate a question?

00:55:51.180 --> 00:55:53.143
- So according to the Smithsonian,

00:55:54.050 --> 00:55:58.560
in 1963 television host and
erstwhile actor Merv Griffin

00:55:58.560 --> 00:56:02.230
was flying back to New York
City with his wife Julann

00:56:02.230 --> 00:56:04.220
after a weekend visiting
her parents in Michigan.

00:56:04.220 --> 00:56:06.740
Didn't need to add that but they did.

00:56:06.740 --> 00:56:09.017
Merv was looking at
notes for a new game show

00:56:09.017 --> 00:56:12.176
and Julann asked if it was one
of the knowledge-based games

00:56:12.176 --> 00:56:14.463
she likes so is it a trivia game?

00:56:18.810 --> 00:56:21.017
He said, "Since The $64,000 Question,

00:56:21.017 --> 00:56:24.030
"the network won't let you
do those anymore," he said.

00:56:24.030 --> 00:56:26.090
The rigging scandal of the 1950s,

00:56:26.090 --> 00:56:27.800
like that movie Quiz Show.

00:56:27.800 --> 00:56:29.450
- Yeah.
- Which very good movie.

00:56:31.570 --> 00:56:33.870
The scandals had killed
off American quiz shows

00:56:33.870 --> 00:56:36.173
seemingly for good.

00:56:37.030 --> 00:56:40.110
And he said, "They suspect you
of giving them the answers."

00:56:40.110 --> 00:56:41.627
And she said something to the effect of,

00:56:41.627 --> 00:56:43.977
"Well why don't you give them the answers

00:56:43.977 --> 00:56:46.077
"and people make up the questions."

00:56:47.960 --> 00:56:50.640
And he's like thinking
about it, he's like okay,

00:56:50.640 --> 00:56:52.003
the answer is 5,280.

00:56:53.031 --> 00:56:54.320
And he thought for a moment.

00:56:54.320 --> 00:56:56.690
The question is, how many feet in a mile.

00:56:56.690 --> 00:57:00.463
And he's like, the answer
is 79 Whistful Vista.

00:57:02.037 --> 00:57:02.870
The then the response was,

00:57:02.870 --> 00:57:05.670
where did Fibber McGee and Molly live?

00:57:05.670 --> 00:57:07.850
I don't know who Fibber McGee is.

00:57:07.850 --> 00:57:10.403
This was in 1963.

00:57:11.730 --> 00:57:14.943
Those two simple questions
changed TV history.

00:57:15.870 --> 00:57:16.795
There you have it.

00:57:16.795 --> 00:57:20.220
It was just a simple
response but it was such a,

00:57:20.220 --> 00:57:22.403
it was a hook, and then I'm thinking,

00:57:23.380 --> 00:57:26.383
well, if you give an answer,

00:57:27.800 --> 00:57:30.400
you could ask multiple
questions to get that answer.

00:57:32.000 --> 00:57:35.920
